<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    한국어. The same keys as values/strings.xml, in the same order, so the two can be read side by side.

    Korean has no letter case, so the base file's lower-case rule has nothing to act on here; what
    carries over is the brevity. Labels are short nouns, explanations are in 해요체 — the plain polite
    register the rest of the app's voice maps to — and every count is a single <item quantity="other">,
    because Korean does not inflect a noun for number.

    Names of things outside the app (last.fm, genius, lrclib, .lrc, .m3u, android, usb) stay as they
    are written in English, which is how a Korean user meets them on the web and on the phone.
-->
